            <h2 id="stance">Where CorpusWright stands</h2>

            <p>CorpusWright is built on one position: the description of a corpus is not a preliminary step taken before statistical analysis; it is the first stage of statistical analysis. The data-science tradition has a name for this stage: exploratory data analysis (Tukey, 1977), the work of understanding a dataset's structure, its distribution, and its limits before any confirmatory claim is made. CorpusWright's response is not an argument but a default: the descriptive diagnostics are computed for every corpus, in the same way, every time - whether or not anyone planned to look at them.</p>

            <p>The position is not novel, and the project does not claim it as its own. It is the ordinary discipline of open science - reproducible results, transparent methods, findings that carry their provenance, claims held to organised scepticism - applied consistently to one corner of linguistics where it is applied unevenly. In practice, that discipline means five things:</p>

            <ul>
              <li><strong>Deterministic.</strong> The same corpus and the same configuration give the same results, every time. A saved cleaning configuration is part of the research record: it can be inspected, shared, and re-applied without relying on anyone's recollection of manual steps.</li>
              <li><strong>Conservative.</strong> Where a cleaning decision is uncertain, the original text is preserved. Detected noise is evidence, not an instruction; it becomes a cleaning rule only after explicit review. The tool would rather under-clean than silently discard what cannot be recovered.</li>
              <li><strong>Auditable.</strong> Extraction warnings, cleaning rules, and the provenance of every count - the tokeniser version, and for annotated counts the model and its version - remain inspectable, because changing the tokeniser or the tagger changes the counts.</li>
              <li><strong>Local-first.</strong> Private corpora stay on your own machine. Nothing is uploaded anywhere.</li>
              <li><strong>Statistically restrained.</strong> Frequencies are reported as what they are, without overstatement; measures respect corpus design, dispersion, document-level variation, and their own uncertainty.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Nothing new, made routine</h3>

            <p>None of the mathematics in CorpusWright is new: Zipf’s law dates to Zipf (1949), Shannon entropy to Shannon (1948), and the principles of careful corpus design are older still. What is new is the delivery: a local-first tool that computes these diagnostics by default, in the same way, every time.</p>
          </section>

